            <ArticleTitle>Stapled haemorrhoidectomy in Chinese patients: a prospective randomised control study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>373-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare stapled haemorrhoidectomy with open diathermy haemorrhoidectomy in Chinese patients with respect to the postoperative pain, symptom control, and manometric alterations.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective randomised controlled trial.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">A regional general surgical unit, Hong Kong.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty-four patients with second- or third-degree haemorrhoids or who have had failed medical treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Open diathermy haemorrhoidectomy or stapled haemorrhoidectomy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Structured questionnaire for symptoms, anorectal manometry, transrectal ultrasound, and postoperative pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Stapled haemorrhoidectomy compared with open diathermy haemorrhoidectomy resulted in similar postoperative pain and drug requirements. Postoperative control of prolapse symptoms was significantly better with open diathermy haemorrhoidectomy than with stapled. The control of other symptoms was similar with regard to bleeding, pain, pruritus, and incontinence scores. Anorectal manometry showed a decrease in the maximum resting pressure and maximum squeeze pressure in both groups, but the decrease was only significant in the stapled haemorrhoidectomy group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Stapled haemorrhoidectomy is as effective as conventional haemorrhoidectomy for the treatment of haemorrhoids, but with the exception of skin tag prolapse. There is a need for long-term follow-up for the changes in manometric parameters after haemorrhoidectomy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
